foundation.solution_text:
Translate the base graph $\frac{\pi}{2}$ units up.
The horizontal asymptotes become $y=0$ and $y=\pi$.
At $x=0$, $y=\frac{\pi}{2}$, so the $y$-intercept is $\left(0,\frac{\pi}{2}\right)$.
The curve lies strictly above $y=0$, so there is no $x$-intercept.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.7cm,y=1cm]
\draw[->] (-4.6,0)--(4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5)--(0,3.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-4.4,3.1416)--(4.4,3.1416) node[right] {$y=\pi$};
\node[below] at (-3,0) {$y=0$};
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-4.4:4.4,samples=100] plot (\x,{rad(atan(\x))+1.5708});
\fill (0,1.5708) circle (2pt);
\node[left] at (0,1.5708) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Translate the base graph $1$ unit to the right. Its horizontal asymptotes remain $y=\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $y=-\frac{\pi}{2}$.
For the $y$-intercept, substitute $x=0$.
$y=\arctan(-1)=-\frac{\pi}{4}$
For the $x$-intercept, set $y=0$.
$\arctan(x-1)=0$
$x-1=0$
$x=1$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.7cm,y=1.3cm]
\draw[->] (-4.6,0)--(5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.2)--(0,2.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-4.4,1.5708)--(5.4,1.5708);
\draw[dashed] (-4.4,-1.5708)--(5.4,-1.5708);
\node[above] at (-3,1.5708) {$y=\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[below] at (-3,-1.5708) {$y=-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-4.4:5.4,samples=100] plot (\x,{rad(atan(\x-1))});
\fill (1,0) circle (2pt);
\fill (0,-0.7854) circle (2pt);
\node[below right] at (1,0) {$1$};
\node[left] at (0,-0.7854) {$-\frac{\pi}{4}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

